{
  "$comment": "GENERATED by scripts/spec/generate-encoding-cases.mjs; do not edit. Every label of the Encoding Standard (https://encoding.spec.whatwg.org/) with the encoding it names, and every byte of each of the standard's single-byte encodings and of x-user-defined, as every reader of a resource's text must read them. It is the exhaustive half of the rule docs/protocol/W3C-SELECTORS.md states of a resource's text; decode-cases.json, beside it and written by hand, holds what is said of a content type, of a label, of a byte-order mark and of malformed UTF-8 and UTF-16. A CASE is an `encoding`, by its name in lower case, and its `labels`, the name among them. A reader given a content type whose charset is any one of the labels, in any case and with ASCII white space around it, reads the bytes in that encoding. What that is, is one of three things. `everyByte`: the encoding is a single-byte one, and this is the text the 256 bytes 00 to ff, in that order, are: one character to a byte, and U+FFFD where the encoding gives the byte none. `bytes` and `text`: some bytes in hexadecimal and the text they are, which tell UTF-8, UTF-16BE and UTF-16LE from one another, and which only a decoder of each of the standard's seven legacy multi-byte encodings reads; where readers' decoders of those differ, in rarer sequences and in what they make of a fault, is not held here. `refused`: every label of the case is refused, by its name: these are the labels of the standard's `replacement` encoding. Every character here that is not printable ASCII is written as an escape. WHERE IT COMES FROM. The labels are read from the npm package @exodus/bytes, an implementation of the standard, at the version the repository's package.json pins (MIT, Exodus Movement); the characters of each byte, and the text of each case's bytes, are what Node's TextDecoder reads; and the generator refuses to write where the two disagree about a label. RUN BY TypeScript in packages/typescript/core (decode-cases.test.ts), by the Rust SDK (tests/decode_cases.rs), by the Python SDK (tests/test_decode_cases.py) and by the Go SDK (resources_test.go), each for every label of every case. The Rust SDK's reader is encoding_rs, which is the standard as Firefox has it, so its run is the check of this table against a second implementation, label by label and byte by byte. A reader in another language runs it too.",
